\section{Background \& Setup}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Self-Consistency and the Condorcet Independence Assumption}
\label{sec:condorcet}

Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{wang2023sc} samples $K$ reasoning traces and selects the majority answer. The Condorcet Jury Theorem (CJT) \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees majority convergence under independent voters \citep{austensmith1996}, but LLM traces violate this independence. Following \citet{ladha1992correlated} and \citet{kish1965survey}, we define effective sample size using Fleiss' $\kappa$ \citep{fleiss1971kappa} as proxy for intraclass correlation:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:effk}
K_{\text{eff}} = K \,/\, [1 + (K{-}1) \cdot \kappa].
\end{equation} In our experiments (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}), $\kappa = 0.21$--$0.82$ yields $K_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.2$--$3.6$ for $K{=}12$, serving as an ordinal diagnostic of vote diversity rather than a literal effective sample size. This independence limitation motivates our investigation.

\subsection{SICA Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}

Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) extends SC by extracting formal constraints from traces and selecting the answer best supported by the satisfiable subset (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).

\paragraph{Trace generation.} Given question $q$, an LLM generates $K$ traces $\{t_1, \ldots, t_K\}$ at temperature $T = 0.7$. Each trace $t_i$ yields answer $a_i$.

\paragraph{Constraint extraction.}\label{sec:constraint-extraction} The same LLM extracts logical constraints from each trace at $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$. The extraction prompt requests JSON output with constraint type, expression, and Z3-compatible formula (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}); in practice, 99.0\% of extracted constraints are propositional at the Z3 level, with only 1.0\% using true quantifiers. The \emph{extraction rate} is the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint.

\paragraph{Deduplication and MAX-SAT.} Constraints are deduplicated by syntactic equivalence (global average deduplication ratio: 46.0\%); each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of source traces. The objective finds the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3 \citep{demoura2008z3} solves this with a 10-second timeout. Answer assertion constraints ($\texttt{answer\_a} == \texttt{True}$, weight 1) ensure every candidate receives a nonzero base score, making SICA a strict generalization of SC.

\paragraph{Scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$. Each candidate $a$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) - \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace producing answer $a$, and $\alpha = 0.5$. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration condition.} If self-extracted constraints are coupled to their source answer, MAX-SAT retains nearly all ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), and scoring degenerates to:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
equivalent to majority voting. We show in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias} that this degeneration occurs in practice.

\paragraph{The $C_0$ diagnostic.} The multi-candidate rate $C_0 = |\{q : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}| / |\mathcal{Q}|$ is a zero-cost diagnostic: $C_0 < 5\%$ means SICA reduces to SC. $\alpha = 0.5$ is not critical (${\pm}0.49$\,pp across $\alpha \in [0, 1]$).

\subsection{The Symbolic Mirage Hypothesis}
\label{sec:mirage}

We hypothesize that self-extracted symbolic structures are not independent verification signals but projections of the generating model's distributional biases. This \textbf{Symbolic Mirage} is specific to self-extracted constraints; symbolic methods employing independently sourced constraints remain effective (Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}). Unlike general self-correction failure \citep{huang2024selfcorrect}, the symbolic mirage is specific to formal verification: the solver is provably sound, yet its outputs are informationally equivalent to majority voting because self-extracted inputs carry the model's distributional prior. We quantify mirage intensity via the \emph{Bias Ratio}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mirage}
\text{BR}(M,T) = \frac{P(c \text{ supports } a_M \mid a_M \neq a^*)}{P(c \text{ supports } a^* \mid a_M \neq a^*)},
\end{equation}
where $c$ is a self-extracted constraint, $a_M$ the model's answer, and $a^*$ the ground truth. $\text{BR} > 1$ indicates constraints project model bias rather than provide independent verification. The mirage degrades aggregation through two channels: correlated constraints compress $K_{\text{eff}} < 2$ (\emph{direct}; Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}), and answer-coupled constraints collapse MAX-SAT to vote counting (\emph{indirect}; Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). If traces are unfaithful \citep{turpin2024language}, the mirage is inherent to extraction. Two observations motivate the hypothesis: (O1)~BR $> 1.0$ across all tested self-extraction conditions (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}); (O2)~no training-free remediation reduces BR below $1.0$ (\S\ref{sec:remediation})---both initially observed on Mistral-7B and subsequently confirmed across nine models. These lead to a testable prediction: (P1)~structurally independent verification should break SCB (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

\begin{tcolorbox}[enhanced, colback=gray!5, colframe=gray!50, boxrule=0.3pt, left=3pt, right=3pt, top=1pt, bottom=1pt, before skip=4pt, after skip=4pt]
\textbf{Empirical Finding 1} (Self-Extraction Ceiling).\label{prop:ceiling}
For generator $M$ with $\text{BR}(M) > 1$ and oracle improvement $\Delta_\text{oracle}$, self-extracted constraints improve SC by at most $\Delta_\text{max}^\text{self}$, empirically bounded by $\Delta_\text{oracle} / \text{BR}(M)$.
\end{tcolorbox}

\begin{tcolorbox}[enhanced, colback=gray!5, colframe=gray!50, boxrule=0.3pt, left=3pt, right=3pt, top=1pt, bottom=1pt, before skip=4pt, after skip=4pt]
\textbf{Empirical Finding 2} (Capability Gap Condition).\label{cor:capability_gap}
Let $G$ be a generator with SC accuracy $\text{SC}_G$ and let $V$ be an external verifier with standalone accuracy $V_\text{acc}$ and inter-system correlation $\rho_{GV} < \rho_{GG}$. Combo gain $\Delta_\text{combo} - \Delta_\text{SC}$ decreases monotonically with $\text{SC}_G$, with significant gains observed up to $\text{SC}_G \approx 70\%$.
\end{tcolorbox}

\textit{Empirical support.} From Finding~1, adding $V$ with $\rho_{GV} < \rho_{GG}$ increases $K_\text{eff}^\text{combo}$. Empirically, combo gain decreases monotonically with $\text{SC}_G$ ($\rho = 0.986$, $p = 0.006$, $n{=}6$; \S\ref{sec:cross-arch}), with significant improvements even when $\text{SC}_G > V_\text{acc}$.

We also define a Conditional Correction Ratio (CCR, Appendix~\ref{app:cil}) that summarizes these diagnostics.

\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Method.}
\looseness=-1 We test with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, classifying (premise, conclusion) pairs as entailment, contradiction, or neutral. This proof-of-concept is limited to propositional-logic benchmarks whose premise-conclusion structure naturally suits NLI; generalization to non-logical domains remains untested. In combo mode, each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times alongside SC's $K$ votes. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$).

\paragraph{Main result.}
\looseness=-1 DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo $w{=}3$ yields $+2.94$\,pp (n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), combo $w{=}3$ achieves significant gains for Mistral-7B ($+6.67$\,pp, $p < 0.001$) and LLaMA-8B ($+4.17$\,pp, $p = 0.004$); stronger generators show no improvement---while same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). After BH correction ($m{=}12$), Mistral-7B ($w{=}3$, $w{=}5$) and LLaMA-8B ($w{=}1$, $w{=}3$) remain significant.

\paragraph{Why it works.}
\looseness=-1 Our results suggest three conditions jointly enable the escape---structural independence, capability ordering, and task-verifier alignment---expanded in \S\ref{sec:discussion}. NLI classification operates on (premise, conclusion) pairs, a different task from constraint extraction; the success may partly reflect task-format alignment. All three NLI architectures produce gains at $w \geq 3$.

\paragraph{Capability gap hypothesis.}
\looseness=-1 Four generators spanning a 46-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with DeBERTa-large-mnli (55.00\% standalone; Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}): Mistral-7B (SC 39.33\%, below the verifier) gains $+6.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$) and LLaMA-8B (SC 70.00\%, above the verifier) gains $+4.17$\,pp ($p = 0.004$); generators above SC $\approx 74\%$ show no improvement. Per-label analysis confirms complementary error patterns that diminish as the generator improves (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}). The monotonic relationship holds across three NLI architectures and six verifier-generator pairs (Spearman $\rho = 0.986$, $p = 0.006$, $n{=}6$; Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}; Appendix~\ref{app:capability-gap}). A $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix confirms BR $> 1$ in all eight cells ($1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}).

